                    <head>Categories of correspondence</head>
                    <table rows="9" cols="2">
                        <row>
                            <cell xml:space="preserve" role="label">ALS / ALI / AL</cell>
                            <cell role="data">Autograph (handwritten) letter, signed, initialled, or unsigned. A message, including greeting cards and unstamped or unaddressed postcards sent in envelopes, that was sent inside of an envelope is always considered a letter, regardless of length. In cases in which Joyce’s letter is written on an unusual document, e.g., a medical report, or a bank statement, such media are noted. Calling cards are noted below.</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell xml:space="preserve" role="label">APCS / APCI / APC</cell>
                            <cell role="data">Autograph postcard signed, initialled, or unsigned. A handwritten printed postcard sent on its own, with recipient address and postage stamp (no envelope); includes both blank and picture cards.</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell xml:space="preserve" role="label">ALSP / ALIP</cell>
                            <cell role="data">Autograph letter signed or unsigned, sent via pneumatique. A letter transmitted through the pneumatic mail system of Paris during Joyce’s time. Generally, these are distinguished by the use of serrated paper with the instruction “pneumatique” written on the envelope.</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell xml:space="preserve" role="label">ALS (dict) / APCS (dict) etc.</cell>
                            <cell role="data">Autograph letter signed and dictated or autograph postcard signed and dictated. An item of correspondence dictated by Joyce and handwritten by an amanuensis.</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell xml:space="preserve" role="label">ACS / ACI / AC</cell>
                            <cell role="data">Autograph card signed, initialled, or unsigned. A handwritten card, generally an imprinted calling card. When mailed in an envelope like a letter, this would still be considered as a card.</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell xml:space="preserve" role="label">ANS / ANI / AN</cell>
                            <cell role="data">Autograph noted signed, initialed, or unsigned. An unmailed brief handwritten note on a small piece of paper that is neither a letter, postcard nor calling card.</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell xml:space="preserve" role="label">Telegram / cablegram</cell>
                            <cell role="data">A brief message sent via a telegraphic transmission system. The sources for transcription may be either the handwritten version taken at the sending office or the printed version as recorded at the receiving office. Since these texts are never in Joyce’s handwriting, they are transcribed in a standard format that generally does not reproduce spacing or typography.</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell xml:space="preserve" role="label">TLS / TLI / TL</cell>
                            <cell role="data">Typed letter signed, initialed, or unsigned. A piece of correspondence typed by Joyce or by an amanuensis and signed by Joyce, not a transcription done at a later date.</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <cell xml:space="preserve" role="label">TcLS / TcPCS</cell>
                            <cell role="data">Typed copy of a letter signed, typed copy of a postcard signed. A typed copy of a letter or postcard done at a later date and not under the authority of Joyce.</cell>
                        </row>
                    </table>
